# Secrets — never commit. tests/test_env_example.py asserts this rule holds.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Tooling
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Generated artifacts
results/*
# ...except the rendered report. It is the page that actually gets published,
# the image build copies it, and versioning it means the published artifact is
# reviewable and its history visible. Rendering is deterministic and CI
# re-renders to prove this copy is not stale.
!results/report.html
# ...and the evaluation artifacts themselves. These are the project's evidence:
# the final held-out run, its fault arm, and the hosted runs including the one
# that failed. A result nobody can inspect is a claim, not a measurement. The
# per-task .jsonl checkpoints stay out — they are a crash-resume aid and every
# record in them is already in the .json.
!results/held-out-*.json
!results/dev-hosted.json
!results/dev-hosted.metrics.json
# The reservation ledger is committed on purpose. It is the only record that
# the held-out set was spent once, and that a released reservation was
# superseded rather than deleted. Keeping it private would make the rule
# unverifiable by a reader.
reports/
*.gguf
